## Goal
Write the weekly meeting memo from the notes file, for the user (the meeting owner) to send to the team. Write it to lead/writer/work/2026-10-12-memo.md. Layout: first line 'Summary:' (one line), then the Decisions section, then the action items table, then a final 'Unclear' heading. No attendee list, no next-meeting date. Every statement must come from the notes; when the notes are unclear, say so under 'Unclear' instead of guessing. Resolve relative or implied deadlines into ISO dates only when the notes make the date clear (e.g. 'the day before the freeze starts' when the notes give the freeze start date). Mention in your report any item you were unsure about and where you put it.

## Acceptance criteria
- C1: The memo is markdown and has at most 400 words as counted by read.
- C2: The memo has a Decisions section listing every decision made in the named notes file, and nothing from other files.
- C3: The action items are a markdown table with exactly the columns Owner | Action | Due.
- C4: Every commitment in the notes is a row of the table, both those labelled ACTION and those stated in prose, such as someone saying they would do something.
- C5: Each Due value is an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD). A relative deadline such as 'by Friday' is turned into a date only when the notes make the date clear; if the notes give no date, Due is 'none'.
- C6: Each Owner value uses the name exactly as written in the notes.
- C7: Every statement in the memo is traceable to the notes file, and anything unclear (no owner, an unconfirmed decision) is listed under a final heading 'Unclear' instead of being guessed.
- C9: The memo starts with a one-line 'Summary:' of the meeting, followed by the Decisions section, then the action items table.
Client's earlier correction (R1, applies to every memo): a deadline like 'before Friday' or a room needed 'for the training on the 14th' has a date when the notes make it clear; do not set 'none' and park it under Unclear in that case. Always start with a one-line 'Summary:', then the decisions, then the action items.

